<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Song of Songs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Song of Solomon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Songs, also called the Song of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, is a book of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> consisting of love poems in the voices of a woman and her lover. Traditionally attributed to Solomon and read in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as an allegory of the love between God and Israel or Christ and the Church, it is usually dated by scholars to the Persian or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/song-of-songs.jpg" alt="Song of Songs" width="320" height="601" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustave Moreau · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gustave_Moreau_-_Song_of_Songs_(Cantique_des_Cantiques)_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Songs, in Hebrew Shir ha-Shirim, is one of the five megillot, or scrolls, in the Ketuvim, the last section of the Tanakh. Unlike other books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> it is erotic poetry, in which lovers express desire, exchange compliments and invite one another to enjoy. It tells a love story between a woman and her lover through dialogues, dreams and metaphors, and the woman warns the daughters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> not to awaken love before its time. </p><p>Its title follows an idiom of biblical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> that marks the supreme member of a class, as in Holy of Holies, and the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> means a song of, by, for or dedicated to Solomon. It has no plot, but links between its beginning and end form a framework, and scholars have not agreed how to divide it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The text gives no explicit reference to its author, or to when, where or how it was composed. Its superscription calls it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s, but modern scholarship argues that it may contain ancient material while there is no evidence that Solomon wrote it. Since the 1800s most scholars have dated it to the 3rd century BCE, chiefly on its language, whose vocabulary, morphology and syntax point to a date centuries after Solomon. </p><p>It has parallels with Mesopotamian and Egyptian love poetry and with the pastoral poems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theocritus">Theocritus</a>, and proposals range from the 10th to the 2nd century BCE; some scholars doubt that its language demands a date after the Babylonian exile. Scholars also debate whether it is a single poem or an anthology, and some connect it with the sacred marriage of Ishtar and Tammuz.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Most scholars now read the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> as erotic poetry celebrating human love, not as a divine metaphor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> accepted it into the canon and long read it as an allegory of the love between God and Israel; canonicity was tied to its attribution to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabbi-akiva">Rabbi Akiva</a> is reported to have forbidden singing it in taverns while defending its place in scripture. Kabbalah later gave it a mystical reading. </p><p>Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> read it during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a>, a custom first recorded in the 8th century. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> it is viewed as an allegory of Christ and his bride, the Church. It has inspired works by Marc Chagall, Carl Theodor Dreyer, Toni Morrison and John Steinbeck.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Song of Songs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Psalms were traditionally attributed in large part to David, and Proverbs, Ecclesiastes and the <b>Song of Songs</b> to Solomon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Tanakh, is the foundational work of Hebrew literature: the Torah, the historical and prophetic books, the Psalms, the wisdom of Proverbs and Job, and the poetry of the <b>Song of Songs</b>, composed and edited over many centuries and reaching its final form in the last centuries BCE.</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Later English versions include Edwin Arnold's The Indian <b>Song of Songs</b> (1875) and Barbara Stoler Miller's Love Song of the Dark Lord (1977).</blockquote><a href="/songs/gita-govinda">Gita Govinda</a></li><li><blockquote>Its refrain takes a phrase from the <b>Song of Songs</b> (7:12), which the Talmudic sage Rava (Abba ben Joseph bar Ḥama) read, in Tractate Eruvin 21b, as the people of Israel speaking to God.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lekha-dodi">Lekha Dodi</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Song of Songs</b>, also called the Canticle of Canticles or the Song of Solomon, is a biblical poem, one of the five megillot (&quot;scrolls&quot;) in the Ketuvim, the last section of the Tanakh.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-songs">Song of Songs</a></li></ul></section>
